\r\\n        \\r\\n        <h2>\\ud83d\\udd2c Main Parts<\\/h2>\\r\\n        <ul>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Nose & Nasal Cavity:<\\/strong> Air enters and gets filtered, warmed, and humidified<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Trachea (Windpipe):<\\/strong> Main airway that carries air to the lungs<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Bronchi:<\\/strong> Two tubes that branch from trachea into each lung<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Bronchioles:<\\/strong> Smaller branches inside the lungs<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Alveoli:<\\/strong> Tiny air sacs where oxygen enters blood and CO2 leaves<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Diaphragm:<\\/strong> Muscle that helps you inhale and exhale<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li><strong>Lungs:<\\/strong> Main organs of respiration<\\/li>\\r\\n        <\\/ul>\\r\\n\\r\\n        <h2>\\ud83d\\udcd6 Functions<\\/h2>\\r\\n        <ul>\\r\\n            <li>Brings oxygen into the body for energy production<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Removes carbon dioxide (waste product from metabolism)<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Filters and humidifies incoming air<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Helps regulate blood pH balance<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Allows you to speak (air passes through vocal cords)<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Protects against airborne germs and particles<\\/li>\\r\\n        <\\/ul>\\r\\n\\r\\n        <h2>\\ud83d\\udca1 Amazing Facts<\\/h2>\\r\\n        <ul>\\r\\n            <li>Your lungs contain about 600 million alveoli \\u2013 if spread out, they would cover a tennis court!<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>You breathe about 22,000 times per day (over 8 million times per year)<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>The left lung is smaller than the right to make room for your heart<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Lungs are the only organ that can float on water<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>A person breathes about 2,100 gallons of air every day<\\/li>\\r\\n            <li>Your lungs produce about 1-2 liters of mucus daily (you swallow it without noticing)<\\/li>\\r\\n        <\\/ul>\\r\\n\\r\\n        <h2>\\ud83d\\udd17 Related Organs<\\/h2>\\r\\n        <a href=\\\"\\/biology\\/lungs\\\" style=\\\"background: #44aaff; color: white; padding: 8px 20px; border-radius: 20px; text-decoration: none; display: inline-block; margin: 5px;\\\">\\ud83e\\udec1 Lungs<\\/a>\\r\\n        \\r\\n        <h2>\\ud83d\\udd17 Related Systems<\\/h2>\\r\\n        <a href=\\\"\\/biology\\/circulatory-system\\\" style=\\\"background: #44aaff; color: white; padding: 8px 20px; border-radius: 20px; text-decoration: none; display: inline-block; margin: 5px;\\\">\\u2764\\ufe0f Circulatory System<\\/a>\\r\\n    <\\/div>\\r\\n<\\/div>\"},\"elements\":[],\"widgetType\":\"html\"}],\"isInner\":false}]'),(2027,238,'footnotes',''),(2029,237,'_elementor_version','4.1.3'),(2030,237,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed','1'),(2031,237,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed_preview','1'),(2032,237,'_elementor_migrations_state_d2a1','4.1.3:997ef14e505ddd4af49c620835e30da6'),(2033,239,'footnotes',''),(2034,239,'_elementor_edit_mode','builder'),(2035,239,'_elementor_template_type','wp-page'),(2036,239,'_elementor_data',''),(2037,239,'_elementor_version','4.1.3'),(2038,239,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed','1'),(2039,239,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed_preview','1'),(2040,239,'_elementor_migrations_state_d2a1','4.1.3:6235a6c47f65a3aca2fc5a7c56ad64e4'),(2041,240,'footnotes',''),(2042,240,'_elementor_edit_mode','builder'),(2043,240,'_elementor_template_type','wp-page'),(2044,240,'_elementor_data',''),(2045,240,'_elementor_version','4.1.3'),(2046,240,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed','1'),(2047,240,'_elementor_global_class_usage_indexed_preview','1'),(2048,240,'_elementor_migrations_state_d2a1','4.1.3:6235a6c47f65a3aca2fc5a7c56ad64e4'),(2049,237,'_wp_page_template','default'),(2050,237,'_elementor_page_settings','a:1:{s:10:\"hide_title\";s:3:\"yes\";}'),(2051,241,'footnotes',''),(2052,241,'_elementor_edit_mode','builder